drm/apple: expose Colorspace and HDR_OUTPUT_METADATA, opt-in

With appledrm.experimental_hdr=1 every connector gets the standard KMS HDR
properties, "Colorspace" (Default, BT2020_RGB) and "HDR_OUTPUT_METADATA".
As the KMS documentation of Colorspace puts it, they say what userspace
renders, and the driver turns that into what the sink expects. On DCP that
is a per-surface tag: every IOMFB surface carries a colorspace and a
transfer function, and per the commits that introduced the tags the
firmware converts each surface to the display's colour space.

One HDR encoding is accepted: BT2020_RGB with the ST 2084 (PQ) EOTF in type
1 static metadata, which is what wlroots and KWin send. The default
colorspace with no metadata, or with SDR gamma metadata, is SDR as before.
Everything else (wide gamut SDR, HLG, the YCC colorimetries) fails the
atomic check, since nothing is known about how DCP treats it.

In HDR:
 - RGB surfaces are tagged DCP_COLORSPACE_BG_BT2020 and DCP_XFER_FUNC_HDR.
   16 is the value the original DCP reverse engineering named HDR, and
   PQ's code point in ITU-T H.273, as 13 (DCP_XFER_FUNC_SDR) is sRGB's and
   9 (BG_BT2020) is BT.2020's; the driver has never sent it before. YCbCr
   surfaces keep the tags their COLOR_ENCODING gives them.
 - The parser also records each timing's best RGB colour mode of up to
   10 bits with the ST 2084 EOTF (deeper ones may need DSC, as with the
   SDR modes). Where one exists (an external HDR display), the modeset
   drives the link in it, and toggling HDR forces a modeset. Where none
   exists, as on the internal mini-LED panels, whose ColorElements hold a
   single 8-bit RGB SDR-gamma mode, the link stays SDR and only the
   surface tags change, so DCP is left to tone map. Whether it does, and
   whether it drives the mini-LED zones past the SDR backlight level for
   highlights as macOS EDR does, is untested.
 - Toggling HDR adds the CRTC's planes to the commit, so the surfaces are
   resubmitted with their new tags even without a new framebuffer.

The properties are attached at probe, before the device is registered, so
they cannot depend on the colour modes of a display hotplugged later. An
external display without a PQ colour mode gets the internal panel's
treatment rather than a failed commit, which would otherwise lock out an
SDR display connected after an HDR session that left the properties set.

Without the parameter no property is attached, no connector state can ask
for HDR, dcp->hdr stays false, and the colour mode, the surface tags and
the swaps are exactly as before.

